To enable long-horizon tasks through goal chaining, we propose a simple modification to our goal-conditioned Qlearning method. Given a sequence τ0:i, instead of limiting the goal to be within the sequence (i.e., g ∼ (s0, ..., si)), we redefine it to be any state observed in the dataset (i.e., g ∼ D). If g = si (e.g. when the goal is the final state of the sub-sequence) then similarly as before, we label such trajectories with R(τ0:i, si) = 1. Otherwise, since we do not know whether g 6= si can be reached from the states within τ0:i, instead of assigning a constant reward, we set the reward of the final transition to be its Q-value, such that R(si, ai, g) = Qπ(si, ai, g):
